BOUNDARY: The proof separately handles \(x=0\) (series is \(1+0+0+\cdots\), absolutely convergent). For \(x\neq 0\) the absolute value is used, so negative \(x\) is covered without change. The Archimedean property yields an integer \(N\ge 1\) with \(\iota(N)>2|x|\) (strict inequality, no choice problem). The ratio \(a_{n+1}/a_n=|x|/\iota(n+1)\) is justified because \(\iota\) preserves multiplication for positive naturals and \((\,n+1)! = n!\,(n+1)\) in \(\mathbb N\). Strict increase of \(\iota\) (lem‑of‑naturals‑positive) gives \(\iota(n+1)>\iota(N)>2|x|\) for all \(n\ge N\), so the ratio is \(<1/2\). Induction yields \(a_{N+j}\le a_N 2^{-j}\); the geometric series \(\sum 2^{-j}\) converges, and multiplying by the constant \(a_N\) preserves convergence (a routinely closed gap). Direct comparison then gives convergence of the tail, and adding the finite initial segment preserves absolute convergence. Because absolute convergence holds for every real \(x\), the power‑series radius is \(+\infty\). Edge cases such as very large \(|x|\) are handled by choosing a correspondingly larger \(N\); no endpoint, empty domain, or degenerate structural parameter affects the argument.

SOURCES: All eight declared/supplied items were inspected: `def-canonical-natural`, `def-factorial-and-falling-factorial`, `def-real-exponential-function-and-e`, `lem-absolute-convergence‑implies‑convergence`, `lem-of-naturals‑positive`, `thm-direct‑comparison‑test`, `thm-geometric‑series`, `thm‑of‑archimedean`. Their statements, hypotheses, and boundary conventions exactly match the uses in the proof.

